WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.720
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday

00:00:01.720 --> 00:00:04.100
Today I want to talk about death and how to avoid it.

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:06.420
Also, there will be a signing update at the end of this video.

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:07.560
But first: Death.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:08.940
So the other day, I dropped my kids off at camp

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:11.420
and as I was helping them out of the car one of them

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:13.720
sneezed directly onto my hands

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:20.820
And then as I was driving home I could literally feel like the gross aerosol-ized cold virus spit drying on my hands,

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:24.260
so I opened up the center console and started looking around for some Purell in there,

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:28.320
and that was a tremendous and potentially fatal miscalculation.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.920
So, I talk a lot about how human life is getting better,

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.180
and in almost every way, it really is.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:39.380
We are healthier, more prosperous, and less likely to die from violence than at any point in human history.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:42.400
But there are a few ways that human life is actually getting worse,

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:47.000
like wealth inequality, for instance, has been growing, although it's still low by historical standards.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.300
And that's bad, especially for the poor, but also for the whole social order because it inhibits

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:53.420
overall economic growth and socioeconomic mobility.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:57.080
And then we have the so called "preventable death rate" here in the United States.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:00.180
These are deaths that could be prevented with existing technology

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:03.280
by reducing medical errors, or getting people to quit smoking, etc.

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:07.640
Between 2010 and 2014, overall preventable deaths in the U.S. decreased, but preventable deaths

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:13.640
by so called "unintentional injury" increased by 23% which is a lot. So what's going on?

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:16.120
Well, as you can see here, there are three big reasons.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:20.820
First: falls, which is going up mostly because the population of the United States is getting older,

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:23.880
and older people are far more likely to die from falling.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:27.700
And then there is poisoning which is almost entirely drug overdoses.

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:34.120
So, back in 1992 Americans were more than three times as likely to be murdered than they were to die of drug overdoses,

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:39.780
but today, Americans are more than three times as likely to die of drug overdoses than they are to be murdered.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:42.900
That's partly because homicide rates have fallen by almost 40%

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:46.060
but mostly because drug overdoses are skyrocketing.

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:50.820
Drug overdoses are now the leading cause of death for Americans under the age of 50.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:53.880
Now, the drug abuse crisis in the United States is very complex.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:57.360
There's a great Healthcare Triage series about it that I will link to in the dooblydoo below,

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:03.200
but what's clear is that what we are doing about the substance abuse crisis is woefully inadequate.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.940
And I just want to take a quick moment here to say to anyone out there living with substance abuse:

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:11.280
You are valuable and deserve to be loved and supported, and to be well,

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:15.020
and there are lots of people who live wonderful lives in recovery.

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:18.940
I know there are not enough options for treatment, but I hope you will seek help today.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:21.140
I've put some resources in the dooblydoo below.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:26.400
Okay, so lastly we have motor vehicle accidents which brings me back to my Purell miscalculation.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:31.240
The overall death rate from car accidents has fallen a lot since 1975 with improvements to car safety,

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:34.240
but in 2015 it actually rose by 7%.

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:38.720
Some of that is probably attributable to more miles being driven as the economy continued to improve,

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:41.720
but most of it is because of distracted driving.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:46.860
Too many of us are using our phones while we drive, or playing Pokemon GO, or reaching for Purell.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:50.980
I didn't get in a car accident while trying to find that Purell just as most of the time people don't

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:53.180
get in car accidents when they text while driving.

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:58.060
But I nonetheless dramatically increased my risk of dying, and also my risk of killing someone else

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:03.660
far more than whatever infinitesimal risk I have a result of the cold virus on my hands.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:09.100
So I'm making this video mostly as a reminder to myself: when I am driving, the greatest threat to my

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:14.600
health and well being, by far, is not whatever text I just received, or whatever spit is drying on my hands,

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:16.400
it's inattentive driving.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:19.820
Alright, sorry if that sounded like a long PSA, it's just there's so few things in life that we

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:22.040
can actually fix, and this is one of them.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:26.660
Signing update: I have about 34,000 copies to go. 
 That's about 68 hours of signing,

